#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:1
msgid "Sharing `#[pyclass]` types between multiple PyO3 extension modules"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:3
msgid ""
"\\[!WARNING\\] This is an advanced topic which requires reaching deeply into "
"`unsafe` code. PyO3 does not have a stable API for doing this, and the "
"approach documented here may break when updating to newer versions of PyO3. "
"See [issue #1444](https://github.com/pyo3/pyo3/issues/1444) for ongoing "
"discussion about this topic."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:8
msgid ""
"Some Python [extension modules](../glossary.md#extension-module) such as "
"NumPy expose a C API which can be consumed by other extension modules to "
"build functionality which directly exchanges native data without needing to "
"go via Python objects. This allows for higher performance than continually "
"moving data in and out of Python objects."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:11
msgid ""
"It is a common request for PyO3 extension modules to be able to share "
"`#[pyclass]` types (and other native data) across multiple crate/package "
"boundaries in a similar fashion to NumPy. Because PyO3 extension modules are "
"each compiled as individual `cdylib` binaries, they cannot depend on each "
"other in the typical Rust way of adding a Cargo dependency (which typically "
"just includes the dependency statically inside the final compiled binary)."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:14
msgid ""
"Instead, the correct way to share `#[pyclass]` data between separate PyO3 "
"extension modules is to use `#[repr(C)]` Rust types and an FFI-like API to "
"exchange data across the `cdylib` boundaries. The solution in this "
"subchapter will explain how to do this correctly."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:17
msgid "Quick summary"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:19
msgid ""
"The solution which this subchapter describes shares types across extension "
"module boundaries by defining three Rust crates (see also the [example "
"project](https://github.com/davidhewitt/pyo3-1444-prototype) on GitHub)."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:21
msgid "A crate `base-package-core` contains:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:22
msgid ""
"`#[repr(C)]` Rust types and APIs to be shared with downstream extension "
"modules (not `#[pyclass]` types or `#[pymethods]`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:23
msgid ""
"An API struct which contains function pointers to the API functions, version "
"information, and any additional data."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:24
msgid ""
"A global static variable which stores the API struct, and a function to "
"initialise this at runtime by importing from the `base_package` Python "
"package. The implementations of the APIs defined in step 1 will delegate to "
"the function pointers in the API struct."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:26
msgid ""
"A crate `base-package` which is a PyO3 project containing `#[pyclass]` thin "
"wrappers around the types exposed in `base-package-core`."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:27
msgid ""
"Implement the API functionality defined in `base-package-core` and populate "
"the API struct accordingly."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:28
msgid ""
"As part of the Python module, export the API struct inside a Python "
"`capsule` object. The initialisation function defined in `base-package-core` "
"will import this capsule and populate the global static copy of the API "
"struct backing the shared APIs."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:31
msgid ""
"A crate `derived-package` which is a PyO3 project depending on `base-package-"
"core` (not `base-package`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:32
msgid ""
"As part of the Python module, import the API struct from the `capule` "
"exposed by the `base_package` Python package and store it in `derived-"
"package`'s copy of the global static variable defined in `base-package-core`."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:33
msgid ""
"Use the APIs from `base-package-core` to implement functionality which "
"directly uses the shared types."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:35
msgid ""
"The sections below go into more detail about how to implement various parts "
"of this solution and why the solution is architected this way."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:37
msgid "Technical background & limitations"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:39
msgid ""
"The Python ecosystem provides a well-established mechanism for sharing C "
"APIs between native extension modules using [Python `capsule` objects]"
"(#capsules). This avoids traditional \"dynamic linking\" between native "
"extension modules, which would rely on system-specific behavior to locate "
"and load `base_package` during import of `derived_package` and to resolve "
"any errors in version mismatch between the two packages."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:42
msgid "The `capsule` mechanism works as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:44
msgid ""
"`base_package` defines a `#[repr(C)]` \"API\" struct which is exported in a "
"Python `capsule` at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:45
msgid ""
"`derived_package` delegates to Python's extension loading mechanism to "
"locate `base_package` and load the API from the capsule."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:46
msgid ""
"`derived_package` then contains its own logic to [check a compatible version]"
"(#api-versioning) of `base_package` was loaded; this is necessary to ensure "
"safe exchange of the API struct."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:48
msgid ""
"When sharing types between multiple PyO3 extension modules through a "
"`capsule`, the complexity arises from two main sources:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:50
msgid ""
"Each Rust extension module may be built with completely separate Rust "
"toolchains and build settings."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:51
msgid ""
"This means anything which is implementation-defined, such as the layout of "
"`#[repr(Rust)] struct`s, the implementation of `std`, and even "
"optimizations, might disagree between the two extension modules."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:52
msgid ""
"Each Rust extension module contains a full statically-linked copy of its own "
"dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:53
msgid ""
"Any `static` global variables which are compiled in the `base-package` will "
"have a **completely independent** copy in the `derived-package`. This "
"includes all `std` globals such as the [global allocator](https://doc.rust-"
"lang.org/stable/std/alloc/trait.GlobalAlloc.html) and [panic hook](https://"
"doc.rust-lang.org/stable/std/panic/fn.set_hook.html)."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:55
msgid ""
"Any dependency version mismatches might mean that bugs in dependencies of "
"`base-package` may not reproduce in the copy in `derived-package`, (e.g. if "
"the common dependency `base-package-core` depends on `foo` 0.1, it is "
"possible `base-package` will compile with `foo` 0.1.1 and `base-package-"
"core` will compile with `foo` 0.1.2)."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:57
msgid ""
"Practically speaking, this introduces the following limitations on extension "
"modules wanting to share data in this way:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:59
msgid ""
"Extensions must take extreme care to ensure that only `#[repr(C)]` types are "
"shared across the package boundary."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:61
msgid ""
"In particular the default `#[repr(Rust)]` layout has no stability guarantee; "
"_two extension modules sharing a `#[repr(Rust)]` data type is undefined "
"behavior_. It is also very easy to accidentally share `#[repr(Rust)]` types, "
"see the [safety note on the `PyCapsule` type documentation](https://pyo3.rs/"
"main/doc/pyo3/types/struct.PyCapsule.html) for cases to consider when "
"sharing data."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:64
msgid ""
"\\[!WARNING\\] > Beware that PyO3's error type, `PyErr`, is not `#[repr(C)]` "
"and cannot be shared across the package boundary > This is an easy mistake "
"to make when exposing fallible APIs which cross the boundary. > There is a "
"[later section on error handling](#error-handling) which suggests "
"alternative strategies"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:66
msgid ""
"APIs which rely on global variables will not work as expected across the "
"package boundary. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:68
msgid ""
"The `#[global_allocator]` used by each extension will likely be different - "
"each will need to ensure that any allocations are freed by the same "
"allocator."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:69
msgid ""
"The `std::io` locks (e.g. for `stdout`) will not be shared, so concurrent "
"output from the two extensions may interleave in unexpected ways."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:71
msgid ""
"PyO3 currently stores `#[pyclass]` types as global variables in static "
"storage in each `cdylib` crate which compiles them. This means that directly "
"sharing a `#[pyclass]` type across multiple `cdylib` crates will currently "
"silently create multiple distinct Python types. To avoid this, the shared "
"types cannot be `#[pyclass]`, instead the package exporting the type to "
"Python needs to make private `#[pyclass]` which wraps the shared type. PyO3 "
"may remove this limitation in future."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:76
msgid "Using a capsule to create a shared API"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:78
msgid ""
"Let's start with the example of NumPy as an extension which wants to offer a "
"C API for other native extensions to consume. The section above already "
"established that Python native extensions can reuse data using the Python "
"`capsule` type. It is an opaque wrapper which can be used to exchange "
"arbitrary native data between Python extension modules. PyO3 provides the "
"[`PyCapsule` type](https://pyo3.rs/main/doc/pyo3/types/struct.PyCapsule."
"html) to create and consume capsules from Rust code."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:83
msgid ""
"NumPy creates a `capsule` which contains a pointer to the structure mapping "
"the implementation of the [\"NumPy C API\"](https://numpy.org/doc/stable/"
"reference/c-api/index.html). While the [exact contents of this structure are "
"generated](https://github.com/numpy/numpy/"
"blob/40a1b0283beacb0d723f51e2046c8dc049da6eee/numpy/_core/code_generators/"
"generate_numpy_api.py#L202-L210), the resulting API structure looks "
"something like the following C code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:87
msgid ""
"// The API is defined as a fully type-erased array of \"void pointers\".\n"
"//\n"
"// This copy of the array is not public API, but internal to the NumPy "
"implementation.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:91
msgid "// Some fields contain function pointers, type erased\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:93
msgid "// Some fields are empty\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:95
msgid "// Some fields point to Python type objects\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:98
msgid ""
"// ... the real API is a few hundred elements long. contents and length are "
"version specific.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:102
msgid ""
"To consume this array from downstream C projects, NumPy also defines a C "
"header file which uses C macros to define the downstream API in terms of "
"cast indexing into this API structure:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:106
msgid "// cast function pointers back to their correct type\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:109
msgid "// cast type objects back to `PyTypeObject *`\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:112
msgid ""
"// downstream packages must call this function before using any of the other "
"APIS\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:116
msgid "/* ... */"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:121
msgid ""
"To expose an API from Rust code, we'll need to take a similar approach. We "
"have the choice of either matching NumPy and using an array of opaque "
"pointers, or using a more typed API `struct` (\\[as long as it is "
"`#[repr(C)]` - see the safety docs on the [`PyCapsule` type](https://pyo3.rs/"
"main/doc/pyo3/types/struct.PyCapsule.html)). A typed `struct` helps to avoid "
"mistakes in casting fields to the wrong type incorrectly, however additional "
"care needs to be taken to ensure that the layout of the struct does not "
"change incompatibly across non-breaking versions of the API. This is "
"discussed further in [the next section](#api-versioning)."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:126
msgid ""
"The snippets below sketch out what the NumPy approach looks like in Rust, "
"using either an array of opaque pointers or a typed API struct. In both "
"cases the public API functions are thin wrappers around the function "
"pointers in the API struct, to provide ergonomics similar to the C macros in "
"the NumPy example."
msgstr ""

