#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:336
msgid "Creating a shared `#[pyclass]` type"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:338
msgid ""
"Once the version checks are complete and the API struct loading is "
"established, this technique can be expanded to share `#[pyclass]` types "
"across the package boundary."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:340
msgid ""
"For a type named `SharedType`, the steps to achieve this are as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:342
msgid ""
"`base-package-core` defines a `#[repr(C)]` struct which contains the data to "
"be shared across the boundary."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:345
msgid ""
"The `BaseApi` struct defined in the previous sections is extended to include "
"functions to manipulate this struct (these functions will later be provided "
"by `base-package`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:347
msgid "At a minimum, this will probably include:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:349
msgid ""
"`get_shared_type: extern \"C\" fn() -> Py<PyType>` - a function to get the "
"`#[pyclass]` Python type object for `SharedType`."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:351
msgid ""
"`create_shared_type: unsafe extern \"C\" fn(SharedType) -> "
"Option<Py<SharedType>>` - a function to create a new instance of the "
"`SharedType` struct and return it as a Python object."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:353
msgid ""
"This function is `unsafe` because the caller must ensure that the thread is "
"[attached](../glossary.md#attached) to the interpreter (`Python<'py>` is a "
"zero-sized type and not FFI-safe)."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:355
msgid ""
"The return type on this function is wrapped in `Option` to allow for failure "
"- see [the error handling section](#error-handling) for more details."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:357
msgid ""
"`cast_shared_type: for<'a> extern \"C\" fn(Borrowed<'a, '_, SharedType>) -> "
"&'a SharedType` - a function to extract a reference to the `SharedType` Rust "
"struct from inside a Python object."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:359
msgid ""
"With the API struct extended to include these functions, the `base-package-"
"core` crate can now implement PyO3 traits for `SharedType` in terms of those "
"functions."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:361
msgid "The crucial traits are:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:363
msgid ""
"`PyTypeInfo` - the `get_type` function can delegate to the `get_shared_type` "
"function pointer in the API struct."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:365
msgid ""
"`IntoPyPyObject` - the `into_pyobject` function can delegate to the "
"`create_shared_type` function pointer in the API struct."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:367
msgid ""
"`FromPyObject<'_>` - the `extract` function can delegate to the "
"`cast_shared_type` function pointer in the API struct."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:370
msgid ""
"`base-package` implements a `#[pyclass]` which is a thin wrapper around the "
"`SharedType` struct, defining its Python functionality."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:372
msgid ""
"`base-package` implements the functions defined in the API struct to "
"manipulate the `SharedType`, and populates the API struct with pointers to "
"these functions as part of creating the `capsule`."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:374
msgid ""
"`derived-package` uses the existing `import_base_package()` function to load "
"the API struct, and then can interact with it as a Python type via PyO3's "
"[smart pointers](../glossary.md#smart-pointer)."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:376
msgid ""
"The [example project](https://github.com/davidhewitt/pyo3-1444-prototype) "
"demonstrates how to do this with a `Series` type implementing a \"mini "
"DataFrame API\", to show how to use these stages to perform real work."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:378
msgid "Practical considerations for sharing data across the package boundary"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:380
msgid ""
"As a reminder, there are two key restrictions to data which is shared across "
"the package boundary:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:382
msgid ""
"Only types with a stable layout, such as `#[repr(C)]` types, can be shared."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:383
msgid ""
"Global variables are not shared across the boundary, and in particular for "
"data sharing, the `#[global_allocator]` is not shared, so data allocated by "
"one package must be freed by the same package."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:385
msgid ""
"This means that all Rust standard library types, such as `Vec`, `String`, "
"and `Box`, cannot be shared across the boundary. Even Rust tuples do not "
"have a stable layout and cannot be shared."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:388
msgid ""
"There [is a proposal for a `#[repr(crabi)]` ABI](https://github.com/rust-"
"lang/rust/issues/111423) which would define stable layouts for many Rust "
"types, which would make it easier to share data. This would still not solve "
"types which contain heap allocations."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:391
msgid ""
"For now, a practical solution is to use the [`abi_stable`](https://github."
"com/rodrimati1992/abi_stable_crates/) crate, which provides many equivalents "
"to Rust standard library types with stable internal layouts. It uses vtables "
"to allow for heap-allocated data to be shared, automatically ensuring the "
"same allocator is used to free data as was used to allocate it. This is the "
"solution used in the [example project](https://github.com/davidhewitt/"
"pyo3-1444-prototype) to share `Vec`\\-like and `String`\\-like data across "
"the boundary."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:395
msgid ""
"Using vtables introduces overhead (e.g. prevents inlining), however this is "
"a necessary consequence of the limitations of sharing data across the "
"package boundary."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:397
msgid "Error handling across the boundary"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:399
msgid ""
"Similar to many other types, PyO3's `PyErr` type is not currently "
"`#[repr(C)]`, so cannot be shared across the package boundary."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:401
msgid ""
"The simplest approach to handling errors across the boundary is to use "
"`Option` return types in the API struct, and to return `None` on error. This "
"is the approach taken in the [example project](https://github.com/"
"davidhewitt/pyo3-1444-prototype)."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:404
msgid ""
"The downside of this approach is that `?` does not trivially work in the "
"implementation of the API functions. The suggested strategy is:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:407
msgid ""
"Inside the API function implementations, convert `PyResult` to `Option` by "
"using `PyErr::restore` to write the error to the Python [thread state]"
"(https://docs.python.org/3/glossary.html#term-thread-state), and returning "
"`None` on error."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:408
msgid ""
"The wrappers in `base-package-core` which delegate to the API functions can "
"then convert the `Option` back to `PyResult` by using `PyErr::fetch` to read "
"the error from the Python thread state."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:410
msgid "Wrap-up, limitations & future work"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:412
msgid ""
"This subchapter has attempted to detail how to use `capsule` objects to "
"create an API for sharing Rust data between multiple PyO3 extension modules. "
"While complex, this is a workable technique for projects which need to have "
"this functionality."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:415
msgid ""
"PyO3 does not yet offer any particular support for this use case and will "
"likely be unable to provide a fully safe API to achieve this while data "
"exchange is limited to `#[repr(C)]` types with pitfalls such as duplicate "
"global variables. Over time PyO3 might accumulate utilities for common "
"pieces of this process."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:418
msgid "Some places where this process process easier in the future include:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:420
msgid "Better `#[pyclass]` support"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:422
msgid ""
"At the moment the `base-package-core` Rust API cannot use PyO3's "
"`#[pyclass]` macro, because of the global variable backing the `#[pyclass]` "
"implementation being a problem when duplicated into `base-package` and "
"`derived-package`."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:424
msgid ""
"A possible solution is that PyO3 could have an option like "
"`#[pyclass(shareable)]`, which could automatically generate the kind of "
"`PyTypeInfo` / `IntoPyObject` / `FromPyObject` implementations which need to "
"be hand-rolled at present."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:426
msgid ""
"To make this work, PyO3 would probably need to support [module state]"
"(https://github.com/PyO3/pyo3/pull/5600), and require the `base-package` "
"which exports the shared type to manually initialize it and store it in "
"module state."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:428
msgid ""
"There are many open design questions about how to make that work elegantly."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:430
msgid "`#[repr(C)]` error handling"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:432
msgid ""
"At present, PyO3's `PyErr` type is a complex internal state machine which "
"allows for lazy creation of Python exceptions. This was convenient in early "
"implementations of PyO3 but carried internal complexity and overhead."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:435
msgid ""
"PyO3's APIs have been trending in recent years towards allowing Rust code to "
"use Rust error types for full control of overhead, e.g. the `IntoPyObject` "
"and `FromPyObject` traits have a `type Error`, and the `#[pyfunction]` "
"macros accept any `Result<T, E>` as long as the error type implements a "
"conversion to `PyErr`."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:437
msgid ""
"It is likely that PyO3 will eventually transition the `PyErr` type to be a "
"thin wrapper around `Py<PyBaseException>`, which would allow it to have a "
"stable layout and participate in error handling across the boundary. This "
"primarily requires consideration about how to nudge existing dependents of "
"`PyErr`'s \"lazy\" internals towards better practices."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:440
msgid "Better support for stable-layout types"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:442
msgid ""
"The restriction of needing `#[repr(C)]` types to achieve a stable ABI for "
"data sharing creates a lot of friction at the boundary. It can also have "
"implications for both efficiency and implementation of the `base-package-"
"core` types."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:445
msgid ""
"The author's experience is that the Rust compiler doesn't yet have a "
"mechanism to comprehensively lint against accidentally sharing types with "
"unstable layouts across the boundary. There may be value in an upstream "
"effort to implement this so that projects using this `capsule` mechanism can "
"avoid easy mistakes."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:448
msgid ""
"Outside of the Rust project itself, [`abi_stable`](https://github.com/"
"rodrimati1992/abi_stable_crates/) crate is the most complete solution "
"currently available to have a convenient way to correctly create a stable "
"ABI. However, `abi_stable` also appears to be largely unmaintained, so users "
"wanting to depend on its functionality may need to consider reviving or "
"forking it."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced/sharing-types.md:451
msgid ""
"Furthermore, PyO3 currently doesn't implement `FromPyObject` or "
"`IntoPyObject` conversions for `abi_stable` types. This makes working with "
"these types somewhat awkward / inefficient at the Python boundary. PyO3 "
"_could_ add an optional feature for this, however given the API surface "
"involved it would be better to resolve the question of maintenance of "
"`abi_stable` before adding such functionality to PyO3."
msgstr ""
